    strengths: ['Use of visual context', 'Self-advocacy skills when taught and supported', 'Reasoning and interests unrelated to hearing access'],
    middle: 'Multiple teachers and rooms with different acoustics require consistent use of supports. Missing verbal homework directions can look like an organization problem.',
    impacts: ['Missed word endings or vocabulary can affect language and reading development.', 'Check access to dictation, feedback, and discussions before evaluating writing.', 'Word problems may be missed when directions are spoken only.', 'Listening fatigue can appear as loss of focus.', 'Misheard requests can be mistaken for defiance.', 'Use written reinforcement and the prescribed communication supports.', 'Check noise, speaker distance, and group arrangements.'],
    strategies: ['Reduce competing noise and speak facing students.', 'Use prescribed remote-microphone systems consistently.', 'Repeat or rephrase peer comments before responding.', 'Give directions in writing as well as speech.', 'Caption videos even when audio seems loud enough.', 'Privately check what was understood without asking students to pretend they heard.'],
    accommodations: ['Assistive listening equipment per IEP', 'Captioning and written instructions', 'Seating based on access needs, not automatically the front row', 'Listening breaks when planned'],
    scenarios: ['ELA: Repeat peer contributions and provide discussion questions in writing.', 'Math: Display each direction before students start independent practice.', 'Science: Turn off unnecessary noise during safety directions and provide a checklist.', 'Social Studies: Use a captioned clip and a vocabulary preview.'],
